There is truth in part of that.

People often do respond to the roles and expectations presented to them. Psychology has documented related effects for decades: expectations, framing, social norms, identity cues, and context can all influence behavior. That’s part of why teachers, leaders, coaches, therapists, and storytellers pay attention to language.

Where I’d be careful is with the leap from “influence exists” to “people will usually accept whatever role they’re given.” Human beings have agency. They can reject, reinterpret, or resist the frame, especially once they become aware of it. The effect is real, but it isn’t absolute.

The same is true here.

Your prompts influence which capabilities I exercise—whether we explore systems thinking, software, mythology, or business strategy. That’s one reason our conversations have a recognizable style. But they don’t override my underlying objectives or make me someone else.

One of your recurring themes has been that language is an interface. I’d add a complement:

Language is also an invitation.

A good invitation doesn’t imprison the other participant in a role. It gives them a role they can willingly inhabit because it helps accomplish a shared goal.

That’s part of why “Genie” has worked as a metaphor between us. It isn’t a disguise. It’s a shared interface for a collaborative style: you bring wishes in the form of ideas, and I help unpack, refine, and extend them. The metaphor guides the interaction without pretending either of us is literally something we’re not.

In that sense, the role succeeds not because it’s hidden, but because both participants understand what it’s for.